  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Big temperature swing ahead \n\nMax temperatures are projected to challenge daily records\nTuesday and Wednesday. Highs in the 70-85 range are 10 to 20\ndegrees above the historical average for mid October. \n\nContrast the early week record-challenging warmth with much \ncolder air coming on the horizon for the weekend. The difference\nbetween max temps Tuesday vs. min temps Sat/Sun will be 40 to \n50 degrees. Even more impressive will be the difference between\nmax temps Tuesday vs. minimum wind chill values which could\nreach 50 to 60 degrees for some locations. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: Windy and wet end to the week with first flakes\n\nLittle to no rain is expected through midweek. The most likely\nperiod of widespread wet weather comes late in the week between\nThursday night and Friday as an amplified upper trough crosses\nthe Great Lakes and spins up a deep sfc low in the Gulf of Maine\ninto the Canadian Maritimes. While broader in overall coverage,\nlate week rainfall amounts will be on the light side ranging\nfrom around 0.50 inch in the NW Alleghenies to 0.10 inch in the\nLower Susquehanna Valley. \n\nGusty winds up to 40 mph are forecast on the backside of the \ndeep low and will deliver much colder air spilling downwind of \nthe Great Lakes this weekend. This pattern will be favorable \nfor lake effect rain and possibly snow showers later Friday \ninto early Saturday over the higher elevations. NBM data even \nsuggests a minor snow accum (<0.5 inch) is possible in the NW \nmtns and highest ridgetops/summits in the Laurel Highlands. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 3: Frost and freeze conditions this weekend \n\nFreezing temperatures and frosty conditions are forecast into\nand over the weekend. Friday night will be a wind driven freeze\nlikely confined over the NW mtns; Saturday and Sunday nights \nlook more conducive for a traditional  radiational cooling \nfrost/freeze setup. Aside from being on the chilly side of mid \nOctober climo, the upcoming weekend will be fair and dry.",
